WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:16.309
HAASS: Well, we will get started.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:18.210
Welcome to the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.660
I'm Richard Haass.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:25.340
And this is part of a series of events which
are high on my list of favorite events because

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:31.970
it is a book launch and that suggests a book
has been written, which in fact it has, in

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:37.620
this case by Josh Kurlantzick who is a senior
fellow here at the Council on Foreign Relations

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:44.720
and is our expert on all things Southeast
Asia, although in this case he has taken not

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:51.360
a geographic approach, but a functional approach
and is tackling the question of what he describes

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.750
as state capitalism.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.070
Let me make sure that is shown.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:05.320
The subtitle is-OK, this is a New York City
driving exam eye test and I just flunked-"How

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.080
the Return of Statism is Transforming the
World."

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:09.080
Congratulations.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.550
KURLANTZICK: Thank you.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.790
Thank you.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:17.090
HAASS: So Josh and I are going to talk for
a few minutes about the book and then we're

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:25.149
going to open it up to you for your questions,
your comments, above all your applause and

00:01:25.149 --> 00:01:31.040
congratulations because all authors deserve
it and need it.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:37.700
So, Josh, let's start out at the beginning,
at the basic just so we understand exactly

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:45.900
what it is we're talking about and ultimately
reading about, which is, what is state capitalism?

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:46.900
KURLANTZICK: OK.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.430
Well, thanks to everyone for coming.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:53.850
In the book I look at the growth of what I
call modern state capitalism over the last

00:01:53.850 --> 00:02:01.090
15 years, a number of prominent, mostly developing
countries where the state has exerted greater

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.479
control of the economy.

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:09.599
And in the book, to differentiate it from
states where the state accounts for a significant

00:02:09.599 --> 00:02:14.880
percentage of GDP through welfare spending
or entitlements, et cetera, I just look at

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.450
states in which the state has control or de
facto control of one-third of the largest

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:21.520
companies in that country.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:25.900
And that gives these states like China, like
Venezuela, a number of these are in the news

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:32.870
now, not always for such good reasons, like
Thailand, like Malaysia, Russia, give the

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.660
state much more direct leverage over the corporate
sector than they would have if the state accounted

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.140
for GDP through welfare spending, through
defense, et cetera.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.230
HAASS: So this is-we would expect this in
places, say, like a Saudi Arabia where you

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:52.190
have a cash crop, a large, extractive industry,
but you're writing much more broadly, correct?

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:53.190
KURLANTZICK: Right.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.560
I mean, there is quite a wide range in the
book.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.910
There are-there's two spectrums.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:01.790
There is the spectrum of state capitalists
who are highly inefficient, like Russia in

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:06.950
which the state has captured a significant
portion of the corporate sector and has resulted

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:12.410
in an extremely predatory type of pseudo-capitalism,
all the way to ones which are somewhat more

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:18.440
efficient, on the end of Singapore, Vietnam,
Thailand, and even China which, despite significant

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.700
problems, is a much more efficient economy.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:25.910
And the other spectrum is autocratic to democratic,
and there are some overlaps there, but not

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:28.120
only natural resources countries.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:35.530
HAASS: And are we seeing more state capitalism
recently or now?

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.620
If so, why?

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:41.380
And does state capitalism, how would I put
it, does it wax and wane?

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:46.069
If you were going to take a long, historical
take on it, have there been previous periods

00:03:46.069 --> 00:03:52.290
when state capitalism was on rise, and have
there been periods when state capitalism was

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:53.319
on the fall?

00:03:53.319 --> 00:03:56.819
KURLANTZICK: Well, certainly we have seen
different periods.

00:03:56.819 --> 00:04:01.030
But this is a period that has grown since
the late '90s, early 2000s.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:06.900
So there was another period after the Second
World War in which there was an era of import

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.099
substitution, statist economics, et cetera.

00:04:09.099 --> 00:04:13.190
What we're seeing now is a number of trends
that have come together.

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:18.220
One, you have a number of countries, Turkey-Turkey's
not state capitalist-but Turkey, Thailand,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:24.650
Russia, Venezuela, Malaysia, others, leaders
who are kind of, like, elected autocrats.

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:30.370
They were elected in elections that run on
the spectrum of freedom, but they had elections.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:35.650
But they also don't really subscribe to the
constitutional bases of democracy, so they're

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:36.840
elected autocrats.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.140
For them, state capitalism is also a means
of perpetuating their power.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:50.190
Second, you had in the Asian financial crisis
and post-2008 financial crisis significant

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:54.710
weakening of the allure of the Western economic
model or of the free market economic model.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:59.160
Third, you have the example of China as a
kind of counterweight.

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.710
And then fourth, particularly after 2008,
a lot of developing countries, I think, also

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:09.030
saw that doing new bank regulations in Europe
particularly and other reasons, many of their

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:12.990
industries were starved of possible infusions
of capital, and they were looking for other

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.600
places for capital.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:20.620
So there have been previous eras, but this
is an era dating back to the late '90s, early

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.620
2000s.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:22.620
HAASS: OK.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:29.710
You mentioned what I thought you were going
to say, which is 2008 clearly hurt the alternative

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:34.380
model, I guess you'd call it more market capitalism
as opposed to-what is the opposite of state

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:35.380
capitalism?

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:36.380
What do you call it?

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:37.380
KURLANTZICK: Yeah, free market capitalism.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:38.380
HAASS: OK.

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:39.551
KURLANTZICK: And obviously there's some spectrum,
but if you take, say, Hong Kong on one end

00:05:39.551 --> 00:05:43.930
and North Korea on the other or something
like that.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:49.280
HAASS: Yeah, that kind of puts you beyond
the end zones.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.660
Does this suggest-I guess maybe I'll ask the
question this way.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:57.450
Are we at something of an apogee-i.e., if
China were to have some real problems, and

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:02.330
it's obviously slowing considerably, does
this potentially taint the appeal of state

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.930
capitalism, what we're going to see then down
the road, Brazil's got problems, China's got

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:12.580
problems, and so forth, that we think maybe
state capitalism has peaked and in a few years,

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:13.930
you know, you wouldn't be writing this book?

00:06:13.930 --> 00:06:19.110
KURLANTZICK: Well, I think that we are seeing
significant problems in all the different-first

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.020
of all, I don't think the free market model
is the alternative.

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:24.830
This was the alternative, so now we're in
two different potential alternatives.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:29.449
But China has problems, Brazil has problems,
but still China's growth rate over the last

00:06:29.449 --> 00:06:30.590
35 years is staggering.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:37.600
And at the same time, a lot of the problems
of free market capitalism we've seen reflected

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:46.350
along with other trends, but the growth of
populist parties in the West, so both of the

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.910
models, I think, have substantial problems.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:56.210
But the longer that China in particular continues
to grow substantially, the more attractive

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.790
it remains as a model for other countries.

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:03.500
It doesn't necessarily have to grow at the
rates as it did before, but just continuing

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:04.500
to grow.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.900
I can talk about, if people have questions,
there are significant flaws, there's NPLs

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.550
piling up.

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:14.990
But the longer that China continues to grow
and also become more assertive in promoting

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.630
its model, it does-
HAASS: NPLs mean non-performing loans?

00:07:17.630 --> 00:07:18.630
KURLANTZICK: Non-performing loans.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:19.630
HAASS: OK.

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:22.729
KURLANTZICK: China's state banks and state
companies still need substantial reform.

00:07:22.729 --> 00:07:23.729
HAASS: SOEs.

00:07:23.729 --> 00:07:28.650
We can kind of make this like a NATO meeting
and just use acronyms-(laughter)-and all that.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:32.229
Let's talk about Brazil for a second because
it's much in the news.

00:07:32.229 --> 00:07:35.140
You're about to have the entire world focus
on it.

00:07:35.140 --> 00:07:39.910
What are we, roughly, six weeks or so, give
or take, away from the Olympics?

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:44.229
You've got the impeachment trial of the president.

00:07:44.229 --> 00:07:53.060
You've got corruption on steroids, literally
and figuratively I guess I'd say, not bad-(laughter)

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.470
-in Brazil.

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.360
What does that tell us about state capitalism?

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:03.740
Or to ask a question in a more leading way,
is it just about inevitable that state capitalism

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:04.740
leads to corruption?

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.990
Do the two almost inextricably go hand-in-hand?

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:12.990
KURLANTZICK: Probably in the long term, although
there are examples, again on a spectrum there

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:19.160
is not a high level of corruption in Singapore
and Norway, which I also look at in the book.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:23.669
I think that Brazil is an interesting example
because, one, it shows in the long term that

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:27.490
there are significant problems with state
capitalism in terms of graft.

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:30.639
They can be avoided, but it's very difficult.

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:37.199
But on the other hand, even though there has
been significant graft, there hasn't been

00:08:37.199 --> 00:08:41.240
significant pressure in Brazil for a dramatic
shift in economic policy.

00:08:41.240 --> 00:08:45.839
There have been pressures for cleaning up
graft and total disgust, but you're not actually

00:08:45.839 --> 00:08:51.279
seeing a dramatic swing to pressure for complete
disavowal of the Lula/Dilma policies.

00:08:51.279 --> 00:08:57.749
So you have democratically elected politicians
who want to respond to public demand for a

00:08:57.749 --> 00:09:03.490
degree of state intervention, but they're
sort of stuck in a trap there.

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:06.939
HAASS: So let me circle back in a different
way.

00:09:06.939 --> 00:09:11.529
If you were going to choose the places, either
current or historical, where state capitalism

00:09:11.529 --> 00:09:19.600
really looked pretty good in terms of overall
growth, both in the collective sense as well

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.319
as per capita, rising living standards and
all that, like, what would be your-what's

00:09:24.319 --> 00:09:28.199
on the short list of places where state capitalism
seems to have worked?

00:09:28.199 --> 00:09:33.600
KURLANTZICK: It has worked pretty well in
Singapore, although I know there are huge

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:35.019
human rights problems.

00:09:35.019 --> 00:09:39.579
It has worked with the state providing initial
capital in Rwanda.

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:44.080
It has worked quite effectively in Norway
where they combine state companies with a

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:48.220
high degree of free market-type governance.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:51.749
It has worked somewhat effectively in Thailand
and Malaysia.

00:09:51.749 --> 00:09:54.800
It has worked somewhat effectively in Indonesia.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:59.240
And to be fair, it has worked somewhat effectively
for a long period of time in China.

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:03.139
HAASS: Now, most of those, but not all of
those tend to be more towards the developing

00:10:03.139 --> 00:10:05.170
side of the spectrum than mature.

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:11.230
So is there an argument that state capitalism
might lend itself to developing economies,

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:15.880
but at a point at which, say, GDP per capita
is above X, 10,000, I don't know what the

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:19.230
number is, that it no longer makes so much
sense?

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.920
KURLANTZICK: Yeah, I think that that's an
argument, although we're going to see it play

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.600
out in China, which is the biggest example.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.639
In Singapore, when Singapore basically was
divorced from Malaysia, the government needed

00:10:29.639 --> 00:10:33.360
some way to launch a range of industries.

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.639
They were having troubling, they had very
shallow capital markets, no one really wanted

00:10:36.639 --> 00:10:38.059
to invest in Singapore.

00:10:38.059 --> 00:10:44.379
The government used state resources to both
attract investment and to provide some seed

00:10:44.379 --> 00:10:47.860
capital as kind of an angel investor in a
few industries.

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:52.589
Now the government has stepped back somewhat,
and that's kind of the model that a lot of

00:10:52.589 --> 00:10:55.860
other countries are looking for, so that's,
like, the ideal model.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:11:00.200
HAASS: Would Japan have been a country that
at one point you would have described as state

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:04.149
capitalist and has, to some extent, evolved
so it's less so?

00:11:04.149 --> 00:11:05.800
KURLANTZICK: Yeah, probably you would.

00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:10.019
I mean, the government links are a little
bit less clear because of the ways that the

00:11:10.019 --> 00:11:15.209
government, the ministries, and the major
companies interact or are a little bit less

00:11:15.209 --> 00:11:16.290
clear, but yes.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:21.209
HAASS: And what are the biggest present or
historic failures of state capitalism?

00:11:21.209 --> 00:11:25.920
Where has it clearly done little or no good
for the society?

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.480
KURLANTZICK: Well, the most obvious example
and the one that's most worrisome because

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:35.170
you have a combination of predatory capitalism
with one of the world's major military powers

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:36.529
is Russia.

00:11:36.529 --> 00:11:42.339
You have a total failure of using the state
to support innovation.

00:11:42.339 --> 00:11:46.149
You have predation that has crowded out entrepreneurship.

00:11:46.149 --> 00:11:50.290
You have Russian elites who have left, and
you have a very angry nationalism.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:52.170
HAASS: What about Venezuela?

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:55.079
KURLANTZICK: Venezuela I would say would be
number two, just Venezuela.

00:11:55.079 --> 00:12:00.759
Although the collapse of the Venezuelan economy
is problematic, it's not the same as a potential

00:12:00.759 --> 00:12:06.499
collapse of the Russian economy, plus Venezuela
doesn't have nuclear weapons, doesn't have

00:12:06.499 --> 00:12:08.279
a Pacific fleet, et cetera.

00:12:08.279 --> 00:12:12.569
HAASS: I asked you before about corruption
and whether there was something about state

00:12:12.569 --> 00:12:18.839
capitalism that more or less inevitably increased
the odds that corruption would become a significant

00:12:18.839 --> 00:12:21.149
problem.

00:12:21.149 --> 00:12:23.189
What about democracy and human rights?

00:12:23.189 --> 00:12:29.519
To what extent does state capitalism almost
ensure that, how would I put it, that the

00:12:29.519 --> 00:12:32.809
political evolution or development of society
lags?

00:12:32.809 --> 00:12:38.470
KURLANTZICK: I'm not sure that the evidence
is so clear on that as it is on corruption.

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:45.220
If you take an overlap of countries and the
degree of state intervention in the economy

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:51.579
with Freedom House's Freedom in the World
scale, there is not a direct overlap.

00:12:51.579 --> 00:12:56.980
There are countries that have historically
been state capitalist that are democracies.

00:12:56.980 --> 00:13:02.980
There are countries today, even Brazil, which
is, for all of its problems, a relatively

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.999
vibrant democracy.

00:13:04.999 --> 00:13:10.119
The problems that are coming out in Brazil
you wouldn't see exposed in Russia because

00:13:10.119 --> 00:13:12.649
there are no institutions to expose them.

00:13:12.649 --> 00:13:19.171
So I think it's possible that in the hands
of someone like Thaksin Shinawatra, Prime

00:13:19.171 --> 00:13:24.869
Minister Najib of Malaysia, and others, state
enterprises can be deployed to limit political

00:13:24.869 --> 00:13:30.379
opposition, but it's not an exact match, it's
not necessarily so.

00:13:30.379 --> 00:13:40.490
HAASS: When economies that are, quote, unquote,
"state capitalists" in their DNA or nature,

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:46.089
when they run into problems, is the tendency
to become more state or more capitalist?

00:13:46.089 --> 00:13:54.259
KURLANTZICK: I think that that depends largely
on the government and it depends largely on

00:13:54.259 --> 00:13:56.269
the quality of government.

00:13:56.269 --> 00:14:00.309
In Singapore, I think the tendency has become
more capitalist, and I think that that is

00:14:00.309 --> 00:14:02.199
also true in some other countries.

00:14:02.199 --> 00:14:07.209
In China, despite promises by the current
government, the tendency, because they have

00:14:07.209 --> 00:14:12.619
a much greater desire for control, the tendency
is to become more state.

00:14:12.619 --> 00:14:18.309
HAASS: That certainly does these days.

00:14:18.309 --> 00:14:25.499
You're a specialist on Southeast Asia, so
let me combine the two.

00:14:25.499 --> 00:14:28.879
What is your part of the world, what's the
evidence there?

00:14:28.879 --> 00:14:34.170
You mentioned before Indonesia, Thailand,
Vietnam now increasingly as probably entering

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:37.879
the state capitalist space, so what are we
seeing there?

00:14:37.879 --> 00:14:45.279
KURLANTZICK: What we're seeing in Southeast
Asia, as a region that once was considered

00:14:45.279 --> 00:14:51.499
both an economic model and also, in many ways,
a model of democratization, has, with a few

00:14:51.499 --> 00:14:56.759
notable exceptions, significantly regressed.

00:14:56.759 --> 00:15:04.420
And you have in Thailand a military junta
which is increasingly meddling in a lot of

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:05.439
realms of the economy.

00:15:05.439 --> 00:15:10.629
You have in Malaysia real regression democratically
and a government that's also increasingly

00:15:10.629 --> 00:15:14.509
using state enterprises a kind of political
vote banks.

00:15:14.509 --> 00:15:18.790
Singapore is an exception, but you now have
in the Philippines a new president, and it's

00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:24.670
unclear what's going to happen, but he has
vowed to himself kill alleged criminals.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:28.339
It's not a great statement for the rule of
law.

00:15:28.339 --> 00:15:33.309
And he also comes from a background that is
very much inclined towards using state enterprises

00:15:33.309 --> 00:15:36.670
as a political tool.

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:41.860
So you've had a meshing there of some pretty
worrying trends.

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:48.239
HAASS: Are there certain prerequisites that
if they're there increases the odds a country

00:15:48.239 --> 00:15:51.419
will go down this path and if they're absent
it won't?

00:15:51.419 --> 00:15:56.699
For example, India is not particularly going
down this path, so what is it about India

00:15:56.699 --> 00:15:59.600
explains the fact that it has not opted for
state capitalism?

00:15:59.600 --> 00:16:03.610
KURLANTZICK: Well, India did opt for a highly
socialist economy in the past.

00:16:03.610 --> 00:16:04.610
HAASS: Yeah, correct.

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:10.029
KURLANTZICK: And so I think one thing that
is clear is if you try this and it fails people

00:16:10.029 --> 00:16:11.259
will go in a different direction.

00:16:11.259 --> 00:16:17.309
And so India and Indonesia have opted for
less than, say, Malaysia because it had such

00:16:17.309 --> 00:16:19.860
significant failures with it.

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:25.709
In addition, I think in a place like India
or some other countries that have opted away

00:16:25.709 --> 00:16:36.089
from it, the influence of democratic civil
society and democratic civil society that

00:16:36.089 --> 00:16:44.139
has been seen, an interplay between state
capitalism and authoritarian rule in the past,

00:16:44.139 --> 00:16:47.269
so that's one reason why on India.

00:16:47.269 --> 00:16:49.759
But you know, I think there's on one factor.

00:16:49.759 --> 00:16:54.869
But for those who see going down this road
the last 10 or 15 years, I think the model

00:16:54.869 --> 00:17:00.609
of China's success, and China's also become
increasingly interested in promoting and success

00:17:00.609 --> 00:17:03.350
through a range of kind of soft power tools
as well.

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:10.130
HAASS: But if a lot of the pessimists then-I'm
going to open things up in a second-if a lot

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:14.079
of the pessimists turn out to be right about
China, that its real rate of growth now is

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:18.890
way, way low, and even that rate of growth
is largely because of public infusions of

00:17:18.890 --> 00:17:23.610
money rather than real economic growth, then
it suggests to me not only might China be

00:17:23.610 --> 00:17:27.940
in some difficulty, but the model that China's
been so responsible for might be in difficulty.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:29.410
KURLANTZICK: No, I think that's accurate.

00:17:29.410 --> 00:17:33.290
I mean, I think we have to see that play out.

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:34.580
I don't disagree with that.

00:17:34.580 --> 00:17:37.850
I think that we'll have to see how that plays
out.

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:39.830
I mean, there has been a high degree of pessimism.

00:17:39.830 --> 00:17:41.760
I think some of that is warranted.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:45.220
But it's been still a quite impressive model
for some time.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:50.670
HAASS: And lastly, if I were to add up, I'd
have to think about it, but state capitalist

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:56.250
economies in the world and not, it would seem
to me that state capitalism or economies that

00:17:56.250 --> 00:18:00.760
are so defined or described would still be
less than half of the world's economies.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:05.700
Simply the U.S. and Europe alone would pretty
much get you to the 50 percent line and a

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:07.011
few others would get you above it.

00:18:07.011 --> 00:18:08.290
Is that fair?

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:10.890
KURLANTZICK: Yeah, that's fair, it depends
on the growth rate of China.

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:13.410
HAASS: OK, it all comes down to China.

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:14.410
OK.

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:15.410
Why don't I stop?

00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:19.190
Why don't we open it up to you, our members
and guests, to ask your questions?

00:18:19.190 --> 00:18:24.940
What we will do, we have this new invention
called the microphone, so when it comes around

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:28.610
if you want to raise your hand we'll give
it to you, if you'd give us your name and

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:34.350
your affiliation and ask a question of Josh
Kurlantzick.

00:18:34.350 --> 00:18:35.800
That would be great.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:37.170
I should also-I've been remiss.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:42.350
I've not mentioned that this book is published
by Oxford University Press.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:49.110
And even more important, it is for sale approximately
20 yards in that direction, by that happy

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:50.230
gentleman sitting there.

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:56.290
But he was feeling a little bit lonely and
wishes he had more attention and more business.

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:58.180
This is our version of state capitalism.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:18:59.180
(Laughter.)

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:04.430
We are encouraging you to participate in the
local economy here.

00:19:04.430 --> 00:19:05.530
OK.

00:19:05.530 --> 00:19:07.150
So any hands up?

00:19:07.150 --> 00:19:11.690
I see two gentlemen in beige suits, so we'll
get the one closer to me and then the one

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:13.480
farther away from me.

00:19:13.480 --> 00:19:15.070
I don't have my glasses on, I can't take it
that far.

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:19.490
Q: Niso Abuaf, Pace University.

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:25.590
This morning on CNBC, none other than Larry
Fink of Blackrock suggested that the solution

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:31.890
to the current malaise of very low or negative
interest rates is private-public partnership.

00:19:31.890 --> 00:19:35.250
That sounds like state capitalism to me in
the developed world.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:37.530
I was wondering if you have any thoughts about
that.

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:38.790
KURLANTZICK: Sure.

00:19:38.790 --> 00:19:44.360
I mean, I'm not an expert on what's going
to solve negative interest rates, but there

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:46.820
are elements of that.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:52.901
If you go back to Singapore, in the early
days there was a very statist model, and then

00:19:52.901 --> 00:19:57.890
you have now a situation in which there are
some state-public partnerships in a number

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:03.330
of industries the Singapore government believes
are crucial to the future, state-public partnerships

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:07.700
in building infrastructure, which is certainly
something that we need here.

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:13.540
But the state also forces both state companies
and private sector companies to compete quite

00:20:13.540 --> 00:20:17.540
intensively, something that you don't get
in a place like Russia and that you get only

00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:18.540
somewhat in China.

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:24.880
HAASS: Yeah, and depending upon the rules
or constitution, say, of a national or regional

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:28.380
infrastructure bank, they might or might not
qualify.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:32.200
And if you have public-private, almost the
percentage of public and the percentage of

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:38.970
private, both in funding and in who makes
the decisions, so it could also actually be

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:43.040
the opposite.

00:20:43.040 --> 00:20:44.040
To you, Craig?

00:20:44.040 --> 00:20:45.040
Q: Yeah.

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:46.040
Thanks, Richard.

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:47.040
Craig Charney of Charney Research.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:52.040
You know, I remember that when Schumpeter
was writing in the 1940s, the thought was

00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:57.430
that the increasing role of state capitalism
would also be a redistributionist and egalitarian

00:20:57.430 --> 00:20:59.470
force in Western societies.

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:05.120
I'm curious what your thought about the beneficiaries
of state capitalism today is.

00:21:05.120 --> 00:21:09.500
I'm struck by the rollcall of countries that
you mentioned, which sound to me to be those

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:14.270
which have authoritarian populism as their
primary mode of governance.

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:17.680
So would your thought be that state capitalism
is essentially a way that elites are reinforcing

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:19.740
their privileges, or what?

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:24.550
KURLANTZICK: I think that the role is varied.

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:29.680
There are countries that are authoritarian
and some that are hybrid and some that are

00:21:29.680 --> 00:21:33.490
truly democratic, like Brazil and Indonesia.

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:35.880
They're not ideal democracies.

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:43.110
In the worst of them, like Russia, the state
is using state capitalism to reward a small

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:44.650
group of elites.

00:21:44.650 --> 00:21:51.270
In those that are more egalitarian, the state
is not only using state capitalism to reward

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:58.390
a small number of elites, but it is trying
to address inequality in some ways.

00:21:58.390 --> 00:22:06.580
Like, for example, in Singapore and when Thaksin
Shinawatra became prime minister in Thailand,

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:12.750
for whatever his failings, his use of the
economy was indeed designed in part to reduce

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:16.660
inequality, also because that was politically
effective for him.

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:18.160
So I think there's a wide range.

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:24.040
I don't think it's only being used to reward
predatory elites.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:28.830
HAASS: Someone from this side of the room.

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:31.780
Now, we're-yes, you have your hand up?

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:32.780
Yes, ma'am.

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:33.780
KURLANTZICK: Hi, Margaret.

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:34.880
Q: Hi, Joshua.

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:35.880
Congratulations.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:39.260
I haven't read your book, but I look forward
to it.

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:40.790
But I-
HAASS: Margaret, you have to introduce yourself.

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:45.450
Q: I'm Margaret Scott, I'm a journalist, and
I've known Joshua for a long time.

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:48.380
I'm interested in what drew you to this subject.

00:22:48.380 --> 00:22:54.700
Your last book was a very interesting look
at the challenges and the fragility of democratization.

00:22:54.700 --> 00:22:59.780
This book is about the state and that state
capitalism is transforming the world.

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:02.470
What drew you to take a look at that?

00:23:02.470 --> 00:23:09.010
And I'm just wondering because as a lay journalist
that doesn't know much of anything, I see

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:15.560
the nation state as under threat in the world
today or challenged and capitalism challenged,

00:23:15.560 --> 00:23:16.560
too.

00:23:16.560 --> 00:23:20.690
So I'm just interested in what drew you to
this idea of state capitalism becoming so

00:23:20.690 --> 00:23:21.890
much stronger.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:29.640
KURLANTZICK: Well, I think that this was,
in a way, kind of a pseudo-sequel to my previous

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:34.460
book that you mentioned, which it was about
the regression of or at least the stagnation

00:23:34.460 --> 00:23:36.860
of democracy in a number of developing countries.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:43.910
And it's possibly a hope I'll have a trilogy
because I'm interested in the combination

00:23:43.910 --> 00:23:49.310
and not just the regression of democracy or
the failure of democratic institutions, but

00:23:49.310 --> 00:23:54.220
the ability to take advantage of them by authoritarians
and authoritarian populists.

00:23:54.220 --> 00:23:56.080
So that might be coming next.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:58.780
But it basically stems from the previous one.

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:02.930
I was interested in how in a number of these
countries, particularly in Southeast Asia,

00:24:02.930 --> 00:24:09.830
but others, state capitalism was both a predecessor
to the rise of these elected autocrats and

00:24:09.830 --> 00:24:13.270
then often a tool that they were using to
reinforce their rule.

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:15.610
But it wasn't always one or the other.

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:20.690
And there was also these exceptions, like
in India where you have the Licence Raj, you

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:25.190
had quite a long period of socialism, you
had emergency rule, and yet they were able

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:27.140
to escape both of those.

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:32.630
So it sort of came from-in the democracy book,
I looked at India as a counter example where

00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:38.130
democracy had continued to thrive compared
to a lot of other countries.

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:42.520
So the democratic regression sort of led into
this, and I wanted to look at the economic

00:24:42.520 --> 00:24:43.990
side and the way that it played in.

00:24:43.990 --> 00:24:48.960
HAASS: Just so I understand, by the way, you've
got state capitalism, you've got market, you

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:53.800
know, economies, and you have some, you know,
varying degrees of hybrids.

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:58.080
Is there something else, other than these
two poles and hybrids, is there something

00:24:58.080 --> 00:24:59.170
else out there?

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:00.790
KURLANTZICK: Not that I know of.

00:25:00.790 --> 00:25:01.790
HAASS: OK.

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:02.790
OK.

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:05.280
KURLANTZICK: I think that the hybrid can vary,
like a French hybrid, a Nordic hybrid.

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:06.280
HAASS: Yeah.

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:07.810
That's a-(pronounces with an accent)-"hybrid."

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:08.810
(Laughter.)

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:09.810
Steven?

00:25:09.810 --> 00:25:12.090
Q: Looking at-
HAASS: You have a microphone right there.

00:25:12.090 --> 00:25:13.500
Q: Thank you.

00:25:13.500 --> 00:25:20.390
Looking at what we're calling state capitalism
or the hand of the state in an economy where

00:25:20.390 --> 00:25:24.280
markets exist, there's a difference.

00:25:24.280 --> 00:25:31.850
If you look at the earlier generation, France
you just mentioned, most of the GDP was coming

00:25:31.850 --> 00:25:35.480
out of state-owned companies for a very long
time.

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:41.010
And to the amazement of the world and, even
more so to the amazement of the French, they

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:45.590
were growing even a little bit faster than
the Germans, which is not the way the world

00:25:45.590 --> 00:25:47.720
was supposed to be ordered.

00:25:47.720 --> 00:25:52.630
The Italians had something somewhat similar;
the Spanish to a slight extent.

00:25:52.630 --> 00:25:59.150
And these state-owned enterprises did rather
well.

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:05.570
They were market-competitive where there was,
you know, not talking about railways, but

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:11.390
all in all they did very well and they were
the main instruments of post-war modernization.

00:26:11.390 --> 00:26:17.350
And you went over across to Japan, where they
did something very similar without owning

00:26:17.350 --> 00:26:18.350
them.

00:26:18.350 --> 00:26:26.010
I mean, who owned Mitsubishi during the great
growth phase is a reasonably interesting question.

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.920
It certainly was not the government, it was
not outside.

00:26:28.920 --> 00:26:35.740
I think Mitsubishi owned Mitsubishi, and it
raised all kinds of-but it was a different

00:26:35.740 --> 00:26:43.750
form of economic organization and state power
and state intervention in markets did an awful

00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:47.210
lot to propel Japanese growth during the high-growth
period.

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:48.320
HAASS: So your question?

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:57.380
Q: My question is, is this latest generation,
which China is the leading candidate, comes

00:26:57.380 --> 00:27:04.980
a lot out of Japan as well as the ownership,
is this going to go in the ways these others

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:13.390
did, which led to stable democracies in a
very great way when the odds weren't altogether

00:27:13.390 --> 00:27:15.300
there?

00:27:15.300 --> 00:27:24.380
Or is this simply another instrument of, you
know, increasingly totalitarian, authoritarian

00:27:24.380 --> 00:27:25.380
government?

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:26.380
HAASS: What do you think?

00:27:26.380 --> 00:27:28.100
KURLANTZICK: Well, that's a lot of questions
there.

00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:35.590
But I think that the world is-I agree with
the premise initially, but the world is different

00:27:35.590 --> 00:27:36.590
in many ways.

00:27:36.590 --> 00:27:40.620
It's harder for a lot of these state capitalists
to protect their economies in some of the

00:27:40.620 --> 00:27:45.270
same ways because they're already signatory
to a whole range of trade agreements that

00:27:45.270 --> 00:27:52.120
bind them and make them so that many of their
state enterprises can't be protected in that

00:27:52.120 --> 00:27:53.120
way.

00:27:53.120 --> 00:27:57.890
I think that the question of whether China
is going to evolve, some of the countries

00:27:57.890 --> 00:28:01.220
I had mentioned already are relatively democratic.

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:05.880
Whether China is going to evolve, I am not
an optimist that this is going to evolve into

00:28:05.880 --> 00:28:08.150
a democratic system anytime soon.

00:28:08.150 --> 00:28:14.330
I think people have gone broke betting that
it's going to change dramatically anytime

00:28:14.330 --> 00:28:15.330
soon.

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:19.720
I do think that there was a role for the state
in those economies, and some of the ones that

00:28:19.720 --> 00:28:22.930
I mentioned the state has played an important
developmental role.

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:27.520
Many of the states have now reached a certain
GDP level where the hand of the state is not

00:28:27.520 --> 00:28:28.850
necessarily that effective.

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:30.900
HAASS: What about when it comes to innovation?

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:35.810
You know, a lot of it comes out of this country
and we're not an example of state capitalism

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:37.690
for the most part.

00:28:37.690 --> 00:28:43.290
Is there a disconnect or a lack of correlation
between state capitalism and innovation?

00:28:43.290 --> 00:28:45.030
KURLANTZICK: Overall, yes.

00:28:45.030 --> 00:28:49.850
I think that some of the most effective state
capitalists, like Singapore and Norway and

00:28:49.850 --> 00:28:56.440
others, have tried to use small portions of
state funds to set up certain incubators,

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:01.270
use state funds to promote industries that
are going to be important in the future.

00:29:01.270 --> 00:29:02.930
China has done that as well, somewhat.

00:29:02.930 --> 00:29:07.200
But overall, they lack deep venture capital
sectors, venture capitalists are afraid of

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:12.110
the government, and there is a problematic
correlation between state capitalism and indigenous

00:29:12.110 --> 00:29:13.110
innovation.

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:14.110
HAASS: OK.

00:29:14.110 --> 00:29:18.090
We've got time for one or two more.

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:19.170
Sure.

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:25.470
Q: Joshua, is there a correlation between
the underground economy, informal economy,

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:27.360
whatever you want to call it, and state capitalism?

00:29:27.360 --> 00:29:30.890
Or is that just some overlap and some not?

00:29:30.890 --> 00:29:38.350
KURLANTZICK: From my research, I think that
the correlation has more to do with poverty

00:29:38.350 --> 00:29:39.690
and the rule of law.

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:44.590
So the underground economy in Singapore, I
don't actually know about Norway, but I would

00:29:44.590 --> 00:29:49.410
be very surprised if Norway has a large underground
economy, it is very small.

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:55.550
Whereas in Russia and Malaysia, the two largest
sources of illicit capital flight in the world,

00:29:55.550 --> 00:30:01.450
but at the same time, some of the others in
the top 10 of illicit capital flight are not

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:05.990
state economies, they're simply very poor
countries or countries with a very weak rule

00:30:05.990 --> 00:30:07.290
of law.

00:30:07.290 --> 00:30:09.890
HAASS: I saw hands.

00:30:09.890 --> 00:30:11.190
Yes, sir?

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:16.090
Q: My best friend who cares about ethics recommended
your book.

00:30:16.090 --> 00:30:22.970
My question is, does ethics play an important
role between the free market concept and state

00:30:22.970 --> 00:30:24.380
capitalism?

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:29.240
HAASS: Anytime someone recommends your book
you don't want to disagree with the premise

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:30.760
of the question.

00:30:30.760 --> 00:30:31.760
(Laughter.)

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:33.600
KURLANTZICK: Yeah.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:38.900
I think that it's-I'm not an ethicist.

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:47.420
I think that the free market capitalism and
state capitalism are ideas that can be infused

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:50.210
and used in the most effective way possible.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:55.750
In the book at the end I talk about ways to,
if countries are going to employ state capitalism,

00:30:55.750 --> 00:31:02.230
to do so with effective governance in ways
in which the state doesn't predate (sp) on

00:31:02.230 --> 00:31:03.230
citizens.

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:07.140
I don't think there's anything more or less
ethical about either of them, but in either

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:12.630
they can be conformed to ways that are more
beneficial to all.

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:18.830
HAASS: Listening to you talk about your book,
I get the sense that you're not so much saying

00:31:18.830 --> 00:31:26.660
this is necessarily good or bad, it just depends
upon the specific variation of certain tendencies,

00:31:26.660 --> 00:31:28.720
but it's actually a more nuanced sort of argument.

00:31:28.720 --> 00:31:29.730
Is that fair?

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:30.730
KURLANTZICK: Yeah.

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:36.360
I mean, besides the democracy element, I wanted
to write the book because I saw the previous

00:31:36.360 --> 00:31:41.520
literature on state capitalism as very one
way or the other.

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:44.530
And I didn't think that was necessarily accurate.

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:50.490
And I thought that looking at it from a policy
perspective, that looking at with such a broad-brush

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:55.400
approach is problematic, especially since
there are so many actors making policy decisions

00:31:55.400 --> 00:31:59.040
involved, and that it was more nuanced than
had been presented in the past.

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:03.850
HAASS: With that, nuance is always a good
place to stop.

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:05.290
There are copies of the book.

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:06.290
Did I mention that?

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:08.710
There are copies of the book for sale.

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:12.120
Look, what you've heard here tonight, I think,
is a really, I used the word nuance, a really

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:14.110
thoughtful exposition.

00:32:14.110 --> 00:32:17.200
Josh, I think, is a twofer.

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:22.790
He's regional expert, but he's also, with
this book and his previous book, been someone

00:32:22.790 --> 00:32:26.330
who's producing books that are really idea
books.

00:32:26.330 --> 00:32:32.140
And the previous one on the state of democracy,
this, in some ways, on the state of economies

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:35.230
and what are the intellectual directions they
are moving in, and I think he's putting together

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:39.690
a really important series of books and of
intellectual work.

00:32:39.690 --> 00:32:44.460
So I commend anyone here or anyone watching
it to it.

00:32:44.460 --> 00:32:50.840
I really think that, you know, this series
is an important contribution to the field

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:52.230
and to the literature.

00:32:52.230 --> 00:32:57.180
So I want to again congratulate him on what
he's done and urge him to keep doing it.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:33:01.340
KURLANTZICK: OK, thank you.

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:02.380
(Applause.)

00:33:02.380 --> 00:33:05.100
HAASS: There's a reception in the back.

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:06.710
And if I didn't mention it, there's books
in the back.

00:33:06.710 --> 00:33:07.710
(Laughter.)

00:33:07.710 --> 00:33:07.711
(END)

